    bio: "Surena — the name is a title meaning 'the mighty' rather than a personal name — was the general of the Suren clan, one of the seven great noble families of the Parthian Empire, and the man who delivered Rome its most humiliating military defeat since Hannibal crossed the Alps. Born around 84 BC into the highest Parthian aristocracy, he had the hereditary right to place the crown on the head of the Parthian king at coronation — a privilege that spoke to the extraordinary status of his family. He was, by Roman accounts, exceptionally tall, and fond of travelling with a private harem of two hundred concubines and a baggage train that seemed more suited to a monarch than a general.\n\nAt the Battle of Carrhae in 53 BC, Surena was not yet thirty years old, commanding a force of perhaps 10,000 men against Crassus\'s 40,000. He had spent years developing and refining the combined arms tactics — horse archers, cataphracts, and the novel logistical solution of using camels to carry reserve arrows — that would define Parthian military doctrine for the next two centuries. His genius at Carrhae lay not merely in the tactical deployment of these forces but in his understanding of Roman psychology: he knew that an army accustomed to enemies who closed with them and fought hand-to-hand would be psychologically as well as physically undone by an enemy that killed at range and refused to stand still.\n\nThe scale of his victory was unprecedented. Twenty thousand Romans dead, ten thousand captured, the legionary standards seized, and the Roman commander himself killed — Carrhae was a catastrophe on the order of Cannae, and Surena achieved it with a force roughly one-quarter the size of the enemy. He sent the head of Crassus to King Orodes II at the Parthian court, where the king was watching a performance of Euripides' Bacchae — and the actor playing Agave, who enters bearing the severed head of her son Pentheus, reportedly seized Crassus\'s actual head and used it as a prop to delighted royal applause.\n\nThe reward for this extraordinary service was death. Orodes II, reportedly alarmed by Surena\'s fame and the loyalty his victory had generated among the troops, had him executed shortly after the battle. He was perhaps thirty years old. No tomb survives, no personal writings, no likeness confirmed beyond a handful of disputed coins. He exists in history almost entirely through the horrified accounts of his enemies — Plutarch, Cassius Dio, Justin — who could not quite hide their admiration for the young general who had shown the world\'s greatest army what defeat looked like."
